  "title": "How to Recognise Cubic, Reciprocal & Exponential Graphs",
  "content": "<p><strong>Cubic graphs</strong> have the form \\(y = ax^3 + \\ldots\\). A positive \\(a\\) goes from bottom-left to top-right; a negative \\(a\\) goes from top-left to bottom-right. They can have one or two turning points.</p><p><strong>Reciprocal graphs</strong> have the form \\(y = \\frac{a}{x}\\). They have two branches — one in quadrant 1 and one in quadrant 3 (if \\(a > 0\\)). The curve never touches the axes (asymptotes at \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 0\\)).</p><p><strong>Exponential graphs</strong> have the form \\(y = a^x\\) (where \\(a > 0\\)). They always pass through \\((0, 1)\\) and have a horizontal asymptote at \\(y = 0\\). Growth curves increase rapidly; decay curves (\\(0 < a < 1\\)) decrease toward zero.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Sketch \\(y = \\frac{4}{x}\\) for \\(x = 1, 2, 4, -1, -2, -4\\).</strong></p><p>\\(x = 1: y = 4\\), \\(x = 2: y = 2\\), \\(x = 4: y = 1\\), \\(x = -1: y = -4\\), \\(x = -2: y = -2\\), \\(x = -4: y = -1\\)</p><p>Two branches, one in Q1 (positive) and one in Q3 (negative). The curve approaches but never touches the axes.</p>"
 },
